 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Shrimp go rubbery the moment they overcook.</li><li>Started in cold water and heated with it, they can’t get ahead of the water: they finish evenly at <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"temp\" data-u=\"C\" data-a=\"60\" data-s=\"0\">60 °C</span>, stay pink all the way through and stay plump.</li><li>Without a thermometer, pull them as soon as the last grey patch turns pink.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why poach from cold"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Skip the dry brine, poaching and dressing.</li><li>Pat the raw shrimp dry and toss with the grated garlic, ginger and soy sauce from the garlic–ginger list; marinate 10–15 minutes.</li><li>Heat the oil in a frying pan over <b>medium</b> heat, lay the shrimp in one layer and cook about 1–1½ minutes a side, just until pink.</li><li>Medium rather than high keeps the garlic from burning and the shrimp pink rather than brown.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Or: garlic–ginger, pan-cooked"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Same as the garlic–ginger version with the soy + sesame list: marinate 10–15 minutes in the soy sauce and sesame oil, then cook in the neutral oil over medium heat, about 1–1½ minutes a side.</p>",
   "title_html": "Or: soy + sesame, pan-cooked"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>About 4 : 1 mayo to sriracha is mild-medium; go to 3 : 1 for more heat.</li><li>It’s the same sauce as on the <a href=\"tuna-mango-roll.html\">spicy tuna & mango roll</a>.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Spicy mayo ratio"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The bowl is yours.</li><li>The poaching method follows the cold-start technique credited to Serious Eats; the dry brine is scaled down from ThermoWorks’ <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"lb\" data-a=\"2\" data-s=\"0\">2 lb</span> recipe.</li><li>The dressing, marinade and spicy-mayo amounts are this site’s starting points.</li><li>Adjust to taste.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Assumed: the amounts"
  }
 ],
